#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199077} :activity/id #uuid "da7c19c9-a092-5227-8c3d-c60c670a8222" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048206 :topic/id #uuid "8850e3a0-f2fb-5671-843a-13422a22aa79" :topic/math-academy-id 4387} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199560 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $A$ and $B$ are sets, the Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs $(a,b)$ whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\big\\{(a, b) \\: : \\: a \\in A,\\, b \\in B \\big\\}\n$$\n\nMany Cartesian products have natural representations in the Cartesian plane. The most common of these is the Cartesian product $\\mathbb R^2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbb R^2 = \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R =  \\big\\{(x, y) \\: : \\: x,y \\in \\mathbb R \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis infinite set contains all ordered pairs $(x,y),$ where $x$ and $y$ can be any real numbers. In other words, $\\mathbb R^2$ contains every point in the Cartesian plane! \n\n![](images/07/070389fcdb6d69f9aa90dabbd98a0d3461e26d3e3e08b1d53b92e99688c09923.png)\n\nSimilarly, the set\n\n$$\n\\mathbb R^3 = \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R =  \\big\\{(x, y, z) \\: : \\: x,y,z \\in \\mathbb R \\big\\}\n$$\n\ndescribes every point in three-dimensional Cartesian space. \n\n![](images/e7/e74fb5948144bc61173e4de73865cb746278bd31fd87e4abf927fa01821ba936.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c18e6618-573b-5560-8d95-58ed56f05155" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8237 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c7652427-e5ff-5d02-9f23-39039f090aff" :step/math-academy-id 153383 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198272 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198169 :question/id #uuid "bb40c174-3739-50df-835e-5d47c9902d5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13192"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421830}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "2190f1df-5853-5d8c-85e2-33f40f1f0f24" :step/math-academy-id 153384 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199579 :tutorial/content "Cartesian coordinates can also be used to visualize other Cartesian products.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following Cartesian product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^- = \\big\\{(x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in\\mathbb N,\\, y\\in\\mathbb R^-\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis product contains every point in $\\mathbb R^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative real number.\n\nTo visualize this Cartesian product, consider the following subset of our Cartesian product:\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\big\\{ (1,y) \\: : \\: y \\in\\mathbb{R}^- \\big\\} \\subset \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-\n$$\n\nThis subset describes all points in the plane whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative real number. We can represent $S_1$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f6165f66b4149b7b2ea52a3cd686836853d85048ca890e850cb771174a150c57.png)\n\nNext, we consider the subset $S_2,$ given by\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\big\\{ (2,y) \\: : \\: y \\in\\mathbb{R}^- \\big\\} \\subset \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-.\n$$\n\nThis subset describes all points in the plane whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative real number. \n\n![](images/ca/caf2299a2e8009ad5b2776ce67786fa7c7e3bdb57aacbd7df87882f59a2350e2.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $S_3, S_4, S_5,\\ldots,$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N\\times {R}^{-} & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup ⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-$ as the set of vertical rays shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/033c40b86bfd5cebf24d34de644f632213207386c2b67c559af7594468599e1e.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1626e562-8e88-5ff3-bae3-4162438c0086" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8329 :tutorial/title "Cartesian Products of Countably Infinite Sets"} :step/id #uuid "cd371945-c79b-5b78-9017-15c39e4d5ed7" :step/math-academy-id 153387 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198154 :question/id #uuid "eae94514-cd92-5405-90b1-ee09dad18e13" :question/math-academy-id "e-13162"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050088}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389916}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "48b1fb46-7071-5897-9dd7-a47e1d955f6c" :step/math-academy-id 153388 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199584 :tutorial/content "Let's sketch a representation of the set $S= [0,1] \\times [0,1]$ in the Cartesian plane.\n\nThe Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS = [0,1] \\times [0, 1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS=\n\\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in [0,1],\\, y\\in [0,1] \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_0 \\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}0}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS_{\\color{red}0} = \\big\\{ ({\\color{red}{0}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in [0,1]\\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_{\\color{red}0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/1c/1cc30fcc7543bfbbf8c5c59d1436eb3b1a0799d60c9ad1d899957e8fd17d58ab.png)\n\nNow, consider a general $S_{\\color{blue}{i}} \\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some ${\\color{blue}{i}} \\in [0,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS_{\\color{blue}{i}} = \\big\\{ ({\\color{blue}{i}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in [0,1]\\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor each ${\\color{blue}{i}} \\in [0,1],$ $S_{\\color{blue}{i}}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $({\\color{blue}{i}},0)$ to the point $({\\color{blue}{i}},1).$\n\n![](images/28/28b9aa0559635ed4090f10dd0b59f2cbb6c297f8736d9509a637b5d78d27561c.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $I = [0,1]{:}$\n\n$$\nS = \\bigcup_{{\\color{blue}i} \\in I} S_{\\color{blue}i}.\n$$\n\nAs $\\color{blue}i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following rectangle that represents the union $\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{{\\color{blue}i} \\in I} S_{\\color{blue}i}{:}$\n\n![](images/76/76ff48755dbfc3ba1943665d08aa8cfd00948bbc1ddfaab560a0af64f11bae26.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "923fb377-d694-5181-8561-c57612f15054" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8339 :tutorial/title "The Cartesian Product [0,1] x [0,1]"} :step/id #uuid "ef01039c-9cce-558a-8067-2ffba087e848" :step/math-academy-id 153392 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198205 :question/id #uuid "aa337681-5d8c-5ea1-b0fe-5d8cd15040a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13320"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390333}] :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "9ddfd97d-7a1a-59ef-8492-3fbe4088f22f" :step/math-academy-id 153393 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198275 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198206 :question/id #uuid "67569060-2f33-5a8d-ae73-690742d3d318" :question/math-academy-id "e-13321"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389797}] :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "5777ec1b-f050-570f-86e4-1e7bffa77dfb" :step/math-academy-id 153396}] :activity/title "Visualizing Cartesian Products" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198532}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069850} :activity/id #uuid "f7fe334b-af06-545b-8320-d04ce420b4ed" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049179 :topic/id #uuid "e14dc884-2cd6-5b93-999d-54098588a4da" :topic/math-academy-id 434} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069850 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069975 :tutorial/content "When considering the intersection of a line and a reciprocal function, three possibilities can occur.\n\nThe first possibility is that there are two intersection points, as illustrated in the example below.\n\n![](images/00/00f19933ef5f2e7879012dbd9b87fd86964ed44b74e54749dccd2186e02e2a6a.png)\n\nThe second possibility is that there is only one intersection point. For example:\n\n![](images/aa/aabf0d5ea18c01842890b6e470d7c8eaaf36371565ff7c271ea2ab24ccfc9565.png)\n\nThe final possibility is that there are no intersection points. For example:\n\n![](images/da/da7ecba1887505f162144ab3b86e4cca9be104cda7e78ba8fef3d02f10de2abc.png)\n\nTypically, when computing the points of intersection of a reciprocal function and a line, we have to solve a system of two equations. In the first graph that was shown above, this system was\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system of equations can (usually) be transformed into a single rational equation, which can itself be converted into a quadratic equation. For the above system, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{x} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x^2 = 1.\n$$\n\nBy solving the quadratic equation, we can find the intersection points. And like quadratic equations, there can be two, one, or no real solutions. In this case, there are two solutions:\n\n$$\nx^2 = 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x = \\pm 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "70a19ea7-8a66-5b98-b7a2-2312cfe9cf44" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3403 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7b6e2fa7-06cd-5725-9235-7ae5c5dbd6a0" :step/math-academy-id 466045 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069851}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069851 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068884 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068602 :question/id #uuid "ffb2b712-52b9-5a09-9ac4-10a88f72f57e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5193"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fafc8c8-0452-582f-b603-6f633de9a39a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049869}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292932}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of Two Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "674f9cf9-a5de-5045-b6cb-e0e31e295966" :step/math-academy-id 466046 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069852}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069852 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069983 :tutorial/content "Let's now turn our attention to the cases where a line and a reciprocal function intersect at either one point or no points.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find where the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}+1$ and the line $y=2$ intersect. To do this, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\ y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal and solving for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}+1 & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the corresponding $y$-coordinate, we plug $x=1$ into either equation. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$.\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7da7ea3f7e2444197a7bc402309c22fe23d95483909d3d6674645101f036370.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "68835670-e15c-543e-9f1c-ba5dfb7c1823" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4596 :tutorial/title "Intersections of Lines and Reciprocal Functions at One or No Points"} :step/id #uuid "999d3467-9a05-5d20-9fd5-1b8783ff67e3" :step/math-academy-id 466050 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069853}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069853 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068885 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068601 :question/id #uuid "7fb3c0cb-147c-5d02-a470-c86591394f2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5192"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05669abb-838d-5b1c-b97f-833a28b1d413" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050334}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338788}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of One or No Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "663d62ae-d9e6-5064-8a5b-bec6133beefb" :step/math-academy-id 466051 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069854}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068886 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068606 :question/id #uuid "b7b1d18b-256a-5aa4-9bcb-bd62e512231d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5260"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2180b368-597c-5b99-a9ef-346169711637" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294908}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of a Parameter Given Two, One, or No Intersection Points"} :step/id #uuid "c86492a6-57ef-5081-b122-627fc0b573d2" :step/math-academy-id 466054}] :activity/title "Finding Intersections of Lines and Reciprocal Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068965}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065218} :activity/id #uuid "4206463b-9991-5aa1-a8f1-1ddfceac3676" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049593 :topic/id #uuid "030b6230-d213-52b6-874d-139dc628effc" :topic/math-academy-id 314} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065218 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065401 :tutorial/content "Intuitively, a function $f(x)$ is **continuous** if we can draw $y=f(x)$ without taking our pen off the paper. \n\nWe can give a precise mathematical definition of continuity using limits:\n\n> *A function $f(x)$ is **continuous at the point** $\\boldsymbol{x=c}$ if*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c).\n> $$\n\nIn other words, a function is continuous at a point if the limit at the point is equal to the function value at the point.\n\nTo test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist? In other words, we must check that $f(x)$ is defined at $x=c.$\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist? In other words, we have to check that $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to c^{-}}f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to c^{+}}f(x).$\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)?$\n\nIf the answer to all three questions is yes, then the function is continuous at $x=c.$ If the function is not continuous at $x=c,$ we say that it has a **discontinuity** at $x=c.$\n\nThe diagram below shows a function that's continuous at $x=c.$\n\n![](images/54/54ebdb8faa4b1ece5620b817b813780e8abd5cc89cf858eed4cb9ba03f24a046.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(c)$ exists$\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exists $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)$ $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nLet's now see how to use these continuity conditions to check for continuity in a piecewise function." :tutorial/id #uuid "4a7b79ea-d89f-5d21-948b-8a735e641995" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6695 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "755b1ece-f8b7-54b2-8c09-f8c4ab79bfba" :step/math-academy-id 40541 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065222}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065219 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064316 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063916 :question/id #uuid "2b7ec4d6-260d-5fcd-bde2-cb84052868cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-10705"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8edb0a6c-f22f-56cf-9545-2fae93896db2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049040}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275312}] :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity at a Point Using the Definition"} :step/id #uuid "a6c61bd4-670b-5aba-b3d3-f57de24f1e4f" :step/math-academy-id 40542 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065220}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065220 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064317 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063917 :question/id #uuid "a9fce66e-f0e3-50a6-a2d9-0407b9d91d5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10706"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338892}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"} :step/id #uuid "53f7255d-8a19-5fff-954a-4e51d40ed568" :step/math-academy-id 40545 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065221}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065221 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064318 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063918 :question/id #uuid "8f95e034-a0fe-5535-9a25-ce549f29c9d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10707"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050987}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338889}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"} :step/id #uuid "f203cd2d-bb6f-5e30-824f-98ee8ab90684" :step/math-academy-id 40548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065222 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065402 :tutorial/content "Remember that to test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)?$\n\nLet's use these three conditions to show that the following function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 3-x & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe check all three conditions in turn.\n\n- Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)= 1+1= 2.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(3-x) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists: \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 \\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\n- We have that $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 = f(1).$ $\\:\\: {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$ The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c55aa89d279d94b7561f6c24ebdb37bafcf46dbc20edf4f93d515aca7ae4cde6.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5ceec35c-4700-5ccf-a376-7a6c1fcd5e94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6746 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "b7d6d175-7180-513d-95c8-c96d9df56bce" :step/math-academy-id 40849 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065219}}] :activity/title "Defining Continuity at a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064410}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130176} :activity/id #uuid "b8fd4e1c-748f-577d-b086-ba515ee43c96" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050270 :topic/id #uuid "3b3fc28f-36b5-5b87-bb0f-cb9b45688be1" :topic/math-academy-id 2036} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130176 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130445 :tutorial/content "We can describe the **velocity** of a particle moving in the $xy$-plane using a **velocity vector.** The velocity vector tells us the magnitude and direction at which the particle moves. We denote the velocity vector of a particle as $\\mathbf u.$\n\nAs an example, consider the particle $P$ shown below.\n\n![](images/88/8895fcf01aa16812144379c5ae004df694451d6e0a11a2b62e4dd62516f57316.png)\n\nIn this case, the velocity vector is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = (3\\,\\mathbf{i} + 2\\,\\mathbf{j}) \\: \\textrm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nWe should think of the particle's velocity as being made up of two components: \n\n- a *horizontal* component of $3 \\: \\textrm{m/s}$ (moving to the right), and\n\n- a *vertical* component of $2 \\: \\textrm{m/s}$ (moving upward).\n\nIn other words, the particle moves $3$ meters to the right and $2$ units up every second. Also, since the horizontal and vertical components are constants, we say that the particle moves with **constant velocity.**\n\nThe magnitude of the velocity vector gives the **speed** of the particle. Therefore, in our example, the speed of the particle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u| & =|3i+2j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d6f4c5c9-7add-589a-a0ea-d52f3d02e40a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6062 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0f2baea5-8b66-5b0e-b2d7-72a64c3d40a3" :step/math-academy-id 36897 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130177}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130177 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129249 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129069 :question/id #uuid "b776ff14-81d8-5e5d-8ea4-15969eb3904e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9816"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0118bf72-c730-59f1-b722-85ed779f96c7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050988}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398403}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Speed of a Particle Given Its Velocity"} :step/id #uuid "bb3ae2a7-8c5a-5ef2-97b6-41dc1c9886bc" :step/math-academy-id 36898 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130178}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130178 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130446 :tutorial/content "The acceleration of a particle tells us the rate at which its velocity changes over time. We can use vectors to model the acceleration of a particle moving in the $xy$-plane. \n\nFor example, consider a particle $P$ whose acceleration vector $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = (3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j})\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2.\n$$\n\nWe should think of this particle's acceleration as being made up of two components: \n\n- a *horizontal* component of $3 \\: \\textrm{m/s}^2$ (to the right), and\n\n- a *vertical* component of $4 \\: \\textrm{m/s}^2$ (upward).\n\nIn other words,\n\n- the horizontal component of the velocity increases by $3\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ every second, and\n\n- the vertical component of the velocity increases by $4\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ every second.\n\nAlso, since both components are constants, we say that this particle moves with **constant acceleration**.\n\nIf a particle is moving with constant acceleration, the velocity vector $\\mathbf v$ at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{a}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is the velocity of the particle when $t=0$ (i.e., the **initial velocity**).\n\nFor example, suppose that the initial velocity of our particle is given by $\\mathbf{u} = (2\\mathbf{i} + 3\\mathbf{j}) \\, \\textrm{m/s}.$ What is the particle's velocity when $t=2 \\, \\textrm{s}?$\n\nSubstituting our data into the formula above, we obtain that the velocity of the particle will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\  & =(2i+3j)+(3i+4j)(2) \\\\  & =(2i+3j)+(6i+8j) \\\\  & =(8i+11j)\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e71afcde-f4ec-52b0-bc4b-041b528e32f6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6063 :tutorial/title "Relating Velocity and Acceleration"} :step/id #uuid "2d22baec-c09f-5f9f-b2ee-f15b2c144f57" :step/math-academy-id 36900 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130180}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130179 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129250 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129070 :question/id #uuid "33aad515-8d28-57f4-8d3e-448eb905324c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9817"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5146ef7-bd48-5d0d-903d-882d739b92c7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299669}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle"} :step/id #uuid "4f365715-3ac5-589f-a774-e29ae44744a9" :step/math-academy-id 36901 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130181}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130180 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129251 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129071 :question/id #uuid "bf9ee32f-b32c-5acc-90f7-c2daf10b2497" :question/math-academy-id "e-9818"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7439a96f-07b1-5673-a103-af2c63c25d18" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050136}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398410}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Final Velocity of a Particle"} :step/id #uuid "6eef6463-e817-5063-b678-359ac680a832" :step/math-academy-id 36902 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130179}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130181 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129252 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129072 :question/id #uuid "e2aff165-10d7-5d4d-9058-a15121222ebe" :question/math-academy-id "e-9819"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93d45138-b681-55c0-8910-1400bdf717c5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299730}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Time when a Particle Reaches a Particular Velocity"} :step/id #uuid "2334ada5-75ed-5eef-a510-caabce5c0ae4" :step/math-academy-id 36906}] :activity/title "Velocity and Acceleration for Plane Motion" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129375}]}
